candidate.formal_statement_latex:
There exists a cryptographic primitive $\mathcal Z$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there exists a fully black-box reduction of $\mathcal{CRH}$ to the joint primitive $(\mathcal F,\mathcal Z)$; and
\item there exists no fully black-box reduction of $\mathcal{CRH}$ to $\mathcal Z$ alone.
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently: $\mathcal F$ is \emph{fully black-box helpful} for $\mathcal{CRH}$.

candidate.notation_latex:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\lambda\in\mathbb N$ is the security parameter; PPT means probabilistic polynomial time; an \emph{oracle-aided PPT} machine runs in polynomial time relative to every oracle, counting each oracle call as one step.
\item $\mathcal F$ denotes the primitive of one-way functions, $\mathcal{CRH}$ the primitive of collision-resistant hash functions, and $\mathcal Z$ an auxiliary primitive; all three are cryptographic primitives in the sense of the definition below.
\item $(\mathcal F,\mathcal Z)$ denotes the joint primitive whose implementations are pairs $(\mathsf F,\mathsf Z)$ of implementations of $\mathcal F$ and $\mathcal Z$, and which is broken when either component is broken.
\end{itemize}
